WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.400
So, it turns out I have to take a
language credit in university.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.480
So, I'm going to take the easiest
language in the world!

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.084
Good day, everyone.

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:21.345
One of the most common questions
about language learning is:

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.616
"What is the easiest language to learn?"

00:00:23.918 --> 00:00:27.829
Now, there is no exact answer
to that question that applies to everyone,

00:00:27.854 --> 00:00:30.346
because it depends on a few
different factors.

00:00:30.576 --> 00:00:32.290
So, the factors it depends on are:

00:00:32.315 --> 00:00:36.314
your native language, any other
languages you happen to have learned,

00:00:36.339 --> 00:00:39.752
and also the complexity of
the language in question.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.874
So, if your native language is English,

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:44.724
then the easiest language for you

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:48.168
is probably the one that's the
most similar to English.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.569
And if it's not, then maybe you've
learned another language,

00:00:51.594 --> 00:00:53.420
like if you've learned French,

00:00:53.444 --> 00:00:56.259
then maybe the language that's
easiest for you to learn

00:00:56.284 --> 00:00:58.062
is the one that's most similar to French.

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:00.757
Because you've already learned
a lot of the concepts

00:01:00.781 --> 00:01:03.280
that those languages have in common,

00:01:03.305 --> 00:01:06.249
so you have already done a lot
of the work in the past already.

00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:08.795
Okay, so it could be your native language,

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.676
the one that's closest to
your native language,

00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:12.589
closest to other languages you've learned,

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:16.967
and also another factor is the
complexity of that language.

00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:20.078
So, it could be a language
that's similar to your own,

00:01:20.103 --> 00:01:22.878
but if that language happens
to be quite complex,

00:01:22.903 --> 00:01:24.665
then maybe that's not the easiest one.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.912
So it's kind of a balance of
those different factors,

00:01:27.951 --> 00:01:29.847
there's no precise formula,

00:01:29.872 --> 00:01:33.360
but we have an idea just
from those factors.

00:01:34.001 --> 00:01:36.921
Now, the American Foreign Service Institute

00:01:36.946 --> 00:01:39.555
that trains American diplomats 
to go overseas,

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.906
which trains them in language skills
before their placement overseas.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.834
They've done a lot of research into this
topic as it relates to English speakers

00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:51.414
because they're training their
American staff to go abroad.

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:54.016
So, they're English speakers,
but they're learning other languages.

00:01:54.041 --> 00:01:57.152
So, they have lot of data about
how many hours it takes,

00:01:57.177 --> 00:02:02.318
about how intensively they have to study
these different groups of languages.

00:02:02.343 --> 00:02:05.119
So, they have it categorized into
five different groups:

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.143
Category I, which is the easiest languages
to learn for native English speakers,

00:02:09.168 --> 00:02:13.071
then they've got II, III, IV,
and then V is the group

00:02:13.096 --> 00:02:15.359
of the most difficult languages to learn,

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.134
and the people studying those
languages, study for, I believe, 88 weeks.

00:02:20.335 --> 00:02:21.351
Full time.

00:02:21.376 --> 00:02:25.273
So, that's like a year and a half,
something like that.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.146
People who study the
Category I languages,

00:02:28.171 --> 00:02:31.120
I think are in there for
23-24 weeks, full time.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.680
So, it's almost times four if
you learn a level V language.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.314
So, which ones are in level one,
the easiest category.

00:02:39.339 --> 00:02:44.880
They are the Germanic languages,
such as Dutch and Afrikaans.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.522
The Northern-Germanic languages,
from Scandinavia,

00:02:47.547 --> 00:02:52.062
like Swedish, Norwegian, and Danish.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.259
Not Finnish, that's a
separate language group.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.545
And then you've got the
Romance languages like

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:02.820
French, Spanish, Italian and Portuguese,
and I believe also Romanian is in there.

00:03:03.677 --> 00:03:06.464
So, those ones are the most
closely related to English.

00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:10.087
You've got the Germanic ones
like Dutch and Afrikaans,

00:03:10.111 --> 00:03:13.514
and I think those are probably the
most closely related to English,

00:03:13.539 --> 00:03:14.744
and the easiest to learn.

00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:17.062
I haven't actually studied
those personally,

00:03:17.087 --> 00:03:20.640
but a lot of people say that Dutch is
the easiest for English speakers.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.403
I have studied French,

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:27.408
and French has been the easiest language
that I have personally studied.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.780
I used to think it was Indonesian,

00:03:29.805 --> 00:03:32.422
because Indonesian is really simple
and really straightforward,

00:03:32.447 --> 00:03:36.160
and I made really fast progress in it,
and I could communicate in Indonesian

00:03:36.185 --> 00:03:39.296
after studying for just a couple
of months before a trip.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:41.891
But when I started to study French,

00:03:41.916 --> 00:03:45.923
I realized how much of an advantage
that shared vocabulary is,

00:03:45.948 --> 00:03:49.979
because English vocabulary is
something like 35% French,

00:03:50.004 --> 00:03:52.002
so when I would look at a French text

00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:54.185
I would recognise a lot of
the keywords already,

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.899
and I could basically
understand half of the text

00:03:56.924 --> 00:03:59.040
without ever having studied French.

00:03:59.065 --> 00:04:01.682
So, after a few weeks
I could basically read,

00:04:01.707 --> 00:04:04.516
and after a few months I could
communicate and speak.

00:04:04.541 --> 00:04:08.175
Not fluently, but I could
make myself [be] understood,

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.659
and I could understand pretty
much whatever was said to me.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:12.800
Face to face at least.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.473
Not on the radio or not third person,
but face to face I could understand.

00:04:16.498 --> 00:04:19.871
So, French has been definitely
the easiest one for me.

00:04:19.896 --> 00:04:23.046
Category II, by the way,
consists of only German.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.569
German is a Germanic language,
just like Dutch or Afrikaan.

00:04:26.594 --> 00:04:28.239
and it's closely related to English.

00:04:28.525 --> 00:04:32.200
But, it's also quite complex grammatically,

00:04:32.454 --> 00:04:34.855
it's not that complicated
from what I hear,

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.260
but it does have some complications
like grammatical case endings

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:42.029
that other Germanic languages don't have.

00:04:42.054 --> 00:04:44.482
And that's why it's not in Category I,

00:04:44.665 --> 00:04:48.044
it's a little bit more complex than
the Category I languages,

00:04:48.069 --> 00:04:49.728
that puts it in Category II.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.823
And I believe it's the only
language in Category II,

00:04:52.848 --> 00:04:54.942
so it's kind of on its own,

00:04:54.967 --> 00:04:58.320
because it's related to English,
but a little bit complicated.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.488
The hardest has been
Japanese by far.

00:05:02.513 --> 00:05:05.520
And that's one of the
category five languages

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.240
according to the Foreign Service Institute.

00:05:08.385 --> 00:05:10.742
Now, what makes Japanese so hard is,

00:05:10.836 --> 00:05:14.615
first of all, the grammar is
very different from English,

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.354
it's about as different as you can get.

00:05:16.671 --> 00:05:20.457
And also, the writing system
is extremely complex.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.459
And, I think that the FSI
categorisation of level V

00:05:24.484 --> 00:05:26.960
doesn't even take into
account the writing system,

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.210
just the spoken language.

00:05:28.702 --> 00:05:32.059
It matches my experience with
French versus Japanese.

00:05:32.083 --> 00:05:35.566
It feels to me like it took
about five times as long

00:05:35.591 --> 00:05:39.484
to get conversant in Japanese
as it did with French.

00:05:39.683 --> 00:05:41.003
About five times as long.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:45.175
But then, the writing system on top of
that, takes a lot longer than that.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.055
Maybe ten times, twenty times,
thirty times as long

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.564
as it takes to long to read French,
to read and write French.

00:05:52.589 --> 00:05:55.588
Because you're learning four
 different writing systems

00:05:55.613 --> 00:05:56.861
and combining them together.

00:05:56.886 --> 00:05:59.472
You're learning ideographic pictures,

00:05:59.497 --> 00:06:01.670
those are the Chinese characters, Kanji.

00:06:01.813 --> 00:06:03.385
Those are very complicated,

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.447
there's 2000 of them that you need
just to read a book comfortably.

00:06:06.669 --> 00:06:09.342
And they're read in different ways,

00:06:09.367 --> 00:06:11.303
there's different
pronunciations for each one.

00:06:11.454 --> 00:06:14.262
So, learning Japanese is a real
long term intensive thing.

00:06:14.287 --> 00:06:16.168
So, it's been the hardest for me.

00:06:16.366 --> 00:06:18.286
Second to that would be Arabic,

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:21.643
because when you're learning Arabic
you're learning not only one language,

00:06:21.668 --> 00:06:23.287
you're learning the formal language,

00:06:23.312 --> 00:06:27.044
which is kinda outdated and
archaic language.

00:06:27.069 --> 00:06:29.759
But then, you have to learn a
dialect at the same time,

00:06:29.784 --> 00:06:31.647
and then you have to learn
how to communicate

00:06:31.672 --> 00:06:33.040
with speakers of other dialects.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:37.880
So, with Arabic you're learning a whole
language family, that's what makes it hard.

00:06:38.347 --> 00:06:40.532
But, the easiest have
definitely been French,

00:06:40.556 --> 00:06:43.200
followed by Indonesian,
because of its simplicity.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.440
Indonesian, by the way, is a Category
III language according to FSI.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:51.376
Obviously, it's one of the
more simple languages,

00:06:51.401 --> 00:06:56.217
but it doesn't have that shared
vocabulary with English,

00:06:56.242 --> 00:07:00.800
that French or Spanish or Italian
or Dutch or Afrikaans would have.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.016
That's why it's not in Category I,
even though it's very simple.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.240
Anyway, I hope that was helpful,
I hope that gave you an understanding,

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:12.800
and I hope you're not choosing the language
to study just based on how easy it is,

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.840
I hope you're just interested
in getting that information.

00:07:16.109 --> 00:07:18.022
Alright, have a good night.
See you later!

